tal  organic carbon chapter two point two  point four four it emphasizes the  importance of ensuring the complete  oxidation of the organic molecules to be  sure that all organic carbon present is  released and measured accurately most  TOC analyzers used to monitor  pharmaceutical grade water use  ultraviolet light to create hydroxyl  radicals from the water which in turn  oxidize the organic material present  releasing the carbon atoms in the form  of carbon dioxide as I mentioned the  carbon dioxide dissolves into the water  creating carbonic acid which elevates  the conductivity and this change in  conductivity is measured by the TOC  analyzer and used to calculate the  amount of organic carbon present in the  water the problem is that as long  complex chain molecules are broken down  during the oxidation process they may  form organic acids as intermediate  species which in fact will contribute  more strongly to the water conductivity  in the final carbon dioxide does if the  TOC analyzer does not complete the  oxidation of the organic molecule then  one of two things may happen either the  carbon remains bound inside the organic  and cannot be measured so that TOC is  underreported or the TOC analyzer takes  a conductivity measurement before the  complete oxidation when the organic  acids are present and it over approached  the amount of TOC in the water either  way of showing complete oxidation to  carbon dioxide is the only way to  measure TOC accurately  now some TOC analyzers are have a fixed  oxidation time the water continuously  flows through them and is only exposed  to the ultraviolet light for short  periods of time now these can struggle  to report.toc levels accurately the  International Conference on  harmonization is an expert working group  with representation from the European  the United States and the Japanese  pharmacopoeias and in their IC HQ to  document validation of analytical  procedures they recommend a system  suitability test to determine the  robustness of an analyzers measurement  sure the TOC analyzer with fixed  oxidation times be calibrated with a  relatively easy to oxidize TOC standard  such as sucrose and is likely not report  accurately if challenged with a  relatively difficult to oxidize TOC  standard such as the sBBs system  suitability test as recommended in the  Japanese drama compare chapter on TOC  the fix flow path and fixed exposure  times in the UV light will mean the SDB  s will not be fully oxidized and much of  the carbon will remain on measured now  some TOC analyzers employ persulphate as  well as the UV light now the persulphate  extends their measurement range and in  the presence of UV light that  persulphate becomes a very very strong  oxidizing agent enabling the TOC  analyzer to oxidize relatively large  amounts of TOC in a short time however  in designs employing gas selective  membranes as part of the measurement  cell this can present some challenges if  too much of a sulfate is added then  there's a danger that bubbles may form  in a sample when these bubbles can  occlude or block the measurement  membranes leading to underreporting of  TOC levels and equally is too little for  sulfate is added then the TS TOC may not  be fully oxidized and once more the TOC  analyzer will under report the TOC  content in the sample in the  paradoxical situation the analyzer user  somehow has to know in advance the value  of toc in the sample so they can  configure that the sulphate dosing  correctly on the analyzer before they  carry out the measurement so let's take  a look at the pea 8700 TOC and  conductivity analyzer from Beckman  quarter and see how he can help  compliance to the European pharmacopoeia  requirements  now this analyzer is not a  general-purpose analyzer it is designed  specifically for TOC analysis on  purified water and water for injection  and the p80  TOC analyzed from Beckman Coulter  employs the variable oxidation time  combined with a dynamic endpoin